<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Pagus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kadifekale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Pagus, now Kadifekale, is the hill above the harbour of İzmir in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Around 300 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> refounded Smyrna on its slopes, and its summit carried the acropolis of the Hellenistic and Roman city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it held the upper citadel, which the Turks kept while the crusaders of 1344 held the harbour castle below.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-pagus.jpg" alt="Mount Pagus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr. E. Unlu · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kadifekale_IzmirTurkey_EUnluBlogspot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
